[38;2;124;124;124mThe question: does this imply $\int_\Omega u \, dx = 0$?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNote that $|u|^{q-2} u = |u|^{q-1} \text{sgn}(u)$. This is the function $f(u)$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwhere $f(t) = |t|^{q-2}t = |t|^{q-1}\text{sgn}(t)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the condition is $\int_\Omega |u|^{q-1} \text{sgn}(u) \, dx = 0$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe question is whether $\int_\Omega u \, dx = 0$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this. The function $g(t) = |t|^{q-2}t$ is an odd, strictly [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mincreasing function (for $q > 1$). So $g(u) = 0$ in integral means the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m"weighted" average with weight $|u|^{q-2}$ is zero.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me think more carefully. The condition $\int |u|^{q-2} u = 0$ [0m
